<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Claudio Monteverdi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1567–<a href="/years/1643">1643</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Claudio Monteverdi was an Italian composer whose work bridged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> music. After serving the Gonzaga court at Mantua, where he wrote <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/l-orfeo">L'Orfeo</a> (1607), one of the first great operas, he became director of music at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-the-evangelist">St Mark</a>'s Basilica in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> in 1613. In Venice he wrote sacred music, madrigals and late operas, including The Coronation of Poppea, for the new public opera houses.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/claudio-monteverdi.jpg" alt="Claudio Monteverdi" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bernardo Strozzi · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bernardo_Strozzi_-_Claudio_Monteverdi_(c.1630).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Monteverdi was baptised in Cremona on 15 May 1567, the first child of the apothecary Baldassare Monteverdi. There is no clear record of his early training. His first published work, the three-voice motets Sacrae cantiunculae, was issued in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> in 1582 when he was fifteen, and in it he calls himself the pupil of Marc'Antonio Ingegneri, maestro di cappella at Cremona Cathedral, who by one account gave him a solid grounding in counterpoint and composition.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1590 or 1591 Monteverdi entered the service of Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vincenzo-i-gonzaga">Vincenzo I Gonzaga</a> of Mantua as a player of the vivuola, working beside the maestro di cappella Giaches de Wert. He married the court singer Claudia de Cattaneis in 1599. In his last five years at Mantua he completed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/l-orfeo">L'Orfeo</a> (1607) and L'Arianna (1608), and wrote the Messa in illo tempore and the Vespro della Beata Vergine (both 1610). In 1613 he became maestro di cappella of San Marco in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, where he found the choir neglected and had to recruit and manage about 30 singers and six instrumentalists. Much of his Mantuan stage music is lost, perhaps in the wars of 1630. His last years were largely given to opera for Venice.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>He published nine books of madrigals between 1587 and 1638, moving from the style of his teacher to more expressive settings influenced by Wert. He defended novel techniques as a seconda pratica, as against the older prima pratica, and used the basso continuo of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a>. In Il ritorno d'Ulisse in patria and L'incoronazione di Poppea (1643) he helped turn opera from a private entertainment of the nobility into a commercial genre; how much of the surviving Poppea is his is debated. He died on 29 November 1643 and was given a funeral of royal pomp at the Frari. After the 1650s his name faded, and his music was largely forgotten until its rediscovery around the beginning of the twentieth century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Claudio Monteverdi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…centres of Europe: its school of painting included Giovanni Bellini, Giorgione, Titian, Tintoretto and Paolo Veronese, Andrea Palladio built churches and villas that were copied for centuries, and composers from <b>Claudio Monteverdi</b> to Antonio Vivaldi worked in the city, which opened the first public opera house in 1637.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-venice">Republic of Venice · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Cremona, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.13,10.03&amp;year=1616" aria-label="Cremona on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4788 570 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Claudio Monteverdi"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5238 840h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5238 840h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.13,10.03&amp;year=1616">What was here in 1616? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
